---
import StarlightPage from "@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro";
import { Code, Tabs, TabItem } from "@astrojs/starlight/components";
import PageIntro from "../components/PageIntro.astro";
import Illustration from "../components/Illustration.astro";
import SplitSection from "../components/SplitSection.astro";
import NextStep from "../components/NextStep.astro";
import IntegrationMap from "../components/IntegrationMap.astro";
import IntegrationSource from "../components/IntegrationSource.astro";
import { integrationFiles } from "../../scripts/integration-files.mjs";
import data from "../../.generated/site-data.json";
const [javascriptFile] = integrationFiles(data);
const description =
  "Add Tabgrad to your website and run Python or JavaScript on your visitors’ devices.";
---
<StarlightPage frontmatter={{ title: "Get started", description, template: "splash", editUrl: false }}>
  <div class="not-content marketing">
    <PageIntro title="Get started with Tabgrad" description={description}>
      <nav class="project-actions" aria-label="Choose an integration example">
        <a class="text-link" href="#html">JavaScript →</a>
        <a class="text-link" href="#python">Python →</a>
      </nav>
      <Illustration slot="illustration" name="browser" />
    </PageIntro>
    <div class="integration-guide">
      <SplitSection id="html" title="Use Tabgrad from JavaScript">
        <Fragment slot="intro">
          <p>This complete HTML example loads Tabgrad from a CDN and displays a calculation on the page. Use it as a starting point for your own site.</p>
        </Fragment>
        <ol class="setup-steps">
          <li>Save the example as <code>index.html</code>.</li>
          <li>Serve it over HTTPS or localhost and open the page in your browser.</li>
          <li>The page displays <code>Sum: 6</code>. Change the values in the code and reload to calculate a different total.</li>
        </ol>
        <IntegrationSource
          name="index.html"
          href="/examples/javascript/index.html.txt"
          code={javascriptFile.body}
          lang="html"
          description="HTML and JavaScript in one file."
        />
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/reference/browser-package/#use-javascript-directly-in-html">HTML integration guide →</a>
      </SplitSection>
      <SplitSection id="python" title="Use Tabgrad from Python">
        <Fragment slot="intro">
          <p>Pyodide runs Python in the browser. This example connects it to Tabgrad, so you can use <code>import torch</code> without installing Python on your computer.</p>
        </Fragment>
        <ol class="setup-steps">
          <li>Save both files in the same folder.</li>
          <li>Serve the folder over HTTPS or localhost and open <code>index.html</code>.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Run Python</strong> to load the runtime and execute the example.</li>
        </ol>
        <Tabs>
          <TabItem label="index.html">
            <IntegrationSource
              name="index.html"
              href="/examples/python/index.html.txt"
              code={data.integrationPythonHtml}
              lang="html"
              description="The page, its Run Python button and the output."
            />
          </TabItem>
          <TabItem label="python-worker.js">
            <IntegrationSource
              name="python-worker.js"
              href="/examples/python/python-worker.js.txt"
              code={data.integrationPythonWorker}
              lang="javascript"
              description="Loads Python and Tabgrad, and contains the Python code to run."
            />
          </TabItem>
        </Tabs>
        <p>The example prints <code>[-2.0, 4.0]</code>, the gradients calculated by the Python code. To try your own calculation, edit the Python block in <code>python-worker.js</code>, reload the page and run it again.</p>
        <p>Here, <code>import torch</code> uses Tabgrad, not the official PyTorch package. See <a href="/versions/">what’s available</a> before adapting existing code.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/reference/python-host/#run-a-complete-cpu-example">Python integration guide →</a>
      </SplitSection>
      <SplitSection label="Optional" title="Host Tabgrad yourself">
        <Fragment slot="intro">
          <p>You can also serve Tabgrad’s files from your own site instead of a CDN. Install the npm package, then copy its complete <code>dist/</code> folder to a path such as <code>/vendor/tabgrad/</code>.</p>
          <p>Use <code>/vendor/tabgrad/index.js</code> in your import. Keep the supplied folder structure so Tabgrad can find its matching assets.</p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/docs/reference/browser-package/#install-and-serve-your-own-files">Self-hosting guide →</a>
        </Fragment>
        <Code
          wrap
          frame="code"
          title="Get the files with npm"
          code={`npm install @tabgrad/tabgrad@${data.runtimeVersion} --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund`}
          lang="shell"
        />
      </SplitSection>
      <SplitSection title="How Tabgrad runs.">
        <Fragment slot="intro">
          <p>JavaScript and Python use the same Tabgrad engine. These examples run on CPU. You can also use a GPU when the browser and device support it.</p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/docs/architecture/">Explore the architecture →</a>
        </Fragment>
        <IntegrationMap />
      </SplitSection>
    </div>
    <NextStep
      title="Use your GPU."
      description="GPU execution requires a browser and device with WebGPU support. Follow the setup guide for your language."
    >
      <a class="text-link" href="/docs/reference/webgpu-runtime/">JavaScript GPU setup →</a>
      <a class="text-link" href="/docs/reference/python-host/#acquire-a-managed-gpu-connection">Python GPU setup →</a>
      <a class="text-link" href="/versions/">Versions and compatibility →</a>
    </NextStep>
  </div>
</StarlightPage>
